# secrets — never committed, see .env.example
# *.local.md is the key-handoff scratch pad. Listed FIRST and before the file
# is ever created, so there is no window in which a paste could be committed.
*.local.md
# ...and anything DERIVED from it. The original rule matched the file but not
# its editor artifacts: Kate wrote `.SETUP.local.md.kate-swp`, which does not
# match `*.local.md`, and it was committed. That blob turned out to hold no
# secrets, but an editor swap file of a secrets document is a leak waiting to
# happen and "secrets committed to the repo" is an automatic fail.
*.local.md.*
.*.local.md*
# editor and OS scratch files, generally
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.*.kate-swp
*.kate-swp
.#*
\#*\#
.env
.env.local
.env*.local
*.pem
*.key

# deps / build
node_modules/
.next/
out/
dist/
build/
.vercel/
.turbo/

# misc
.DS_Store
*.log
npm-debug.log*
coverage/
.idea/
.vscode/

# typescript incremental build cache
# (next-env.d.ts is intentionally committed: CI typechecks without running
#  next build, so the file must already be on disk)
*.tsbuildinfo
.vercel
.env*
.scratch/
__pycache__/

# One-shot diagnostic scripts written by workers at the repo root. Three have
# appeared and each one turned the commit gate red for whoever committed next.
*.tmp.mjs
*.tmp.js
